 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dfc8sq-gJ-y/",
 "title": "7 Laws of Selling",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator outlines seven fundamental laws of selling to help overcome objections and close deals. The main lesson is to reframe objections, assert expertise, and guide the prospect through questions rather than talking too much.",
 "key_points": [
  "Law of Reframing: Turn the customer's objection (e.g., 'it's too expensive') into the exact reason why they should buy.",
  "Law of Identity: Set expectations and label clients (e.g., as motivated or buyers) so they act in accordance with that identity.",
  "Law of Triad: Ask questions about current problems/stress, transition to future state goals in six months, and finally ask about the cost of inaction.",
  "Law of STFU: Stop talking. The more you talk, the more data points you give them to disagree on.",
  "Law of 10/80/10: Sales resistance is highest at the first 10% and last 10%; the actual sale happens in the middle 80% through deep, question-based conversation.",
  "Law of Inaction: Quantify the cost of inaction because saying 'no' is not staying neutral.",
  "Law of Frame: Hold authority like a doctor prescribing a solution instead of second-guessing."
 ],
 "content": "7 Laws of Selling. 1. Law of Reframing 2. Law of Identity 3. Law of Triad 4. Law of STFU 5. Law of 10/80/10 6. Law of Inaction 7. Law of Frame. The creator claims every reason why somebody wouldn't buy your product or service is actually the reason why they should do it. If they say it's too expensive, you get what you pay for. Turn whatever 'no' they're giving you into the reason why they should buy. Law of identity: if you set an expectation on somebody, they're going to act in accordance. If you tell a client they're a buyer, they're going to act like a buyer. If you label them as motivated, driven, ready to go, seem like you've had enough, they're going to act with urgency when you go to close the deal. The law of triad in this game: ask people questions about where they're at, this is going to make them feel a little bit of stress because they're talking about what they don't like about their current situation. Then you immediately transition to questions about future state, questions about their goals in six months. If everything worked out perfectly, what would that look like? Then you immediately transition to the third part of the triangle, which is questions about cost of an action. In those same six months, if you don't solve the issue, where would you still be? Well, obviously, I'd still be here. Okay, now they're feeling a little bit of inner tension, which is going to make them buy something to ease this tension that they're now feeling. The law of shutting the fuck up: the more you talk, you're just giving them more data points to disagree on. Sales isn't about you, it's about them, so ask questions that way they can yap away. The law of 10/80/10: the first 10% and the last 10% of your sales conversations, sales resistance is the highest. Nobody's going to be sold in the very beginning or the very end right after you show them a price. They're going to be sold in the 80% which is the deep conversation which is question-based selling. Law of inaction: a lot of people assume that if they say no, they're just staying neutral. That is not the case, quantify the cost of inaction. Law of frame: when a doctor prescribes you, you don't sit there with the doctor and say, I don't know, let me think about it, let me do my research, because he's a doctor! He has that expert authority. We are not sales reps, we are experts. Hold frame, ask questions about pain points and offer solutions, offer the prescription.",
 "status": "done",
